Q: How to convert a JavaScript string to ISO-8859-1? A: <p>The ISO-8859-1 (latin-1) encoding code units correspond directly to the first 256 code points of Unicode.  Knowing this, you can use <code>String.prototype.codePointAt</code> for encoding and <code>String.fromCharCode</code> for decoding.  For example like this:</p>
<pre><code>const toLatin1 = (s) =&gt; Uint8Array.from(
  `${s}`, c =&gt; {
    c = c.codePointAt(0);
    if (0 &lt;= c &amp;&amp; c &lt;= 0xff)
      return c;
    throw new TypeError(
      `cannot encode U+${
        c.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(4, '0')} into latin-1`);
  });

const fromLatin1 = (b) =&gt; String.fromCharCode(...Uint8Array.from(b));
</code></pre>
<p>Caveat: without the range check, for strings that cannot be represented in ISO-8859-1, the above would silently truncate the code points modulo 256.  (If you would rather perform lossy encoding, the closest character in meaning to U+FFFD that ISO-8859-1 can represent would probably be U+001A SUBSTITUTE – although C0 control code semantics are not actually part of Unicode, and even the control character names are supplemental.)</p>
<p>You should not use the brain-damaged WHATWG <code>TextEncoder</code> and <code>TextDecoder</code> APIs.  <code>TextEncoder</code> does not support any encoding other than UTF-8 anyway (draft versions allowed specifying the target encoding, but this was later removed and now the encoding parameter is silently ignored); and even if it did, the WHATWG spec redefines the “iso-8859-1” or “latin1” encoding to be another name of Windows code page 1252, which has a different mapping for the [0x80…0x9f] range, creating a nasty surprise when you need to interoperate with something that expects actual ISO-8859-1.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const by = Uint8Array.from(Array(256), (_, i) =&gt; i);
const st = new TextDecoder('iso-8859-1').decode(by);

for (let i = 0; i &lt; 256; ++i) {
  if (st.codePointAt(i) !== i) {
    console.warn(`0x${i.toString(16)} is U+${
      st.codePointAt(i).toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(4, '0')
      } instead of U+${
      i.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(4, '0')}`);
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
